1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,190
Last time we found the top of the hill:

2
00:00:02,230 --> 00:00:05,827
the one setting where every
meter that can level is level.

3
00:00:05,867 --> 00:00:08,499
But we found it by being told where it was.

4
00:00:08,633 --> 00:00:13,109
A computer has no such luck. It
cannot solve for the top with algebra,

5
00:00:13,149 --> 00:00:17,041
because every expected count
passes through the S-curve.

6
00:00:17,081 --> 00:00:20,544
All it can do, from wherever
it stands, is feel the ground:

7
00:00:20,584 --> 00:00:23,724
which way is up, and how
the slope is changing.

8
00:00:23,867 --> 00:00:27,950
So how do you climb a hill you
can only feel one spot at a time?

9
00:00:29,533 --> 00:00:31,754
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

10
00:00:31,794 --> 00:00:33,744
Today: climbing the hill.

11
00:00:35,833 --> 00:00:41,535
Start small, with one slider. Hold
the dial at its fitted place, 2.02,

12
00:00:41,575 --> 00:00:47,831
and stand Dune's slider in the wrong
spot: on Cedar's level, minus 1.52.

13
00:00:47,967 --> 00:00:52,343
Here is Dune's own hill: its height
for every position of its slider.

14
00:00:52,383 --> 00:00:56,076
From where we stand, we can't
see it. We know just two things:

15
00:00:56,116 --> 00:01:00,200
the slope under our feet, and
how fast that slope is changing.

16
00:01:00,333 --> 00:01:04,261
That is enough to draw a parabola
that matches the hill right here.

17
00:01:04,301 --> 00:01:11,478
So jump to the top of the parabola:
0.96 to the right, to minus 0.56.

18
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:18,600
Do it again from the new spot. The second
jump is tiny: 0.017 back to the left.

19
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:22,809
And we are there: minus 0.58. Two steps.

20
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:28,243
Where did 0.96 come from?
Read it off the meter.

21
00:01:28,283 --> 00:01:34,030
The tilt, we know: observed
7, expected 4.8, a gap of 2.2.

22
00:01:34,167 --> 00:01:38,598
The second number is how readily the
expected count moves when the slider does.

23
00:01:38,638 --> 00:01:39,994
Call it the wobble.

24
00:01:40,034 --> 00:01:43,331
A patient near 50% adds the most to it.

25
00:01:43,371 --> 00:01:47,844
A patient near 0 or near
100% hardly adds at all.

26
00:01:47,884 --> 00:01:51,754
For Dune's twelve patients, it comes to 2.29.

27
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:57,108
So the step is tilt over
wobble: 2.2 divided by 2.29.

28
00:01:57,148 --> 00:02:04,239
A gap of 2.2 to close, at 2.29
per unit of slider: move 0.96.

29
00:02:04,367 --> 00:02:07,875
That division is what makes
this more than walking uphill.

30
00:02:07,915 --> 00:02:11,202
It does not just say which
way. It says how far.

31
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:16,419
Now the whole machine, from the plain guess.

32
00:02:16,459 --> 00:02:19,440
Every clinic can take its
step at the same moment,

33
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:23,153
because each step uses only
that clinic's own patients.

34
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:26,823
Then the dial takes one step,
with the sliders frozen.

35
00:02:26,863 --> 00:02:30,405
But that unsettles every clinic,
so the sliders step again.

36
00:02:30,445 --> 00:02:32,738
Then the dial. Then the sliders.

37
00:02:32,867 --> 00:02:40,251
Watch the dial. After eight rounds it has
only reached 1.91, on its way to 2.02.

38
00:02:40,291 --> 00:02:45,569
Each round covers a fraction of what
is left. Getting there takes 54 rounds.

39
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:50,018
And Elm steps down by about one
unit every round, without end.

40
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:55,305
Here is why it is slow. This
is the hill seen from above:

41
00:02:55,345 --> 00:02:59,304
the dial runs left to right,
Birch's slider up and down.

42
00:02:59,344 --> 00:03:04,409
The path is a staircase: up or down
for the sliders, sideways for the dial.

43
00:03:04,533 --> 00:03:09,271
And the ridge it is trying to follow
runs diagonally. Turn the dial up,

44
00:03:09,311 --> 00:03:13,965
and every expected count rises,
so every slider has to come down.

45
00:03:14,005 --> 00:03:17,406
A staircase is a slow way along a diagonal.

46
00:03:17,533 --> 00:03:21,696
How slow depends on something that
should not matter: where we put zero.

47
00:03:21,736 --> 00:03:27,797
Last episode, moving zero changed every
gamma and no gap. It changes the climb too.

48
00:03:27,837 --> 00:03:35,526
Record the risk factor five units higher,
and the same alternation needs 3,125 rounds.

49
00:03:35,667 --> 00:03:40,742
Put zero in the middle of the patients
instead, and the ridge straightens:

50
00:03:40,782 --> 00:03:44,934
21 rounds. That is what
pprof_py does before it starts.

51
00:03:46,533 --> 00:03:48,029
Let's gather it up.

52
00:03:48,069 --> 00:03:52,125
A step is tilt over wobble. It
says which way, and how far.

53
00:03:52,165 --> 00:03:56,093
The sliders can all step at
once. The dial steps in turn.

54
00:03:56,133 --> 00:04:00,152
And taking turns climbs a
diagonal ridge by staircase.

55
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:05,173
Every route here climbs toward the same
top. They differ in how long they take.

56
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:07,209
So here is the obvious question.

57
00:04:07,249 --> 00:04:11,852
Could the sliders and the dial step
together, straight along the ridge?

58
00:04:11,892 --> 00:04:16,701
They can, and then it takes five
steps. Next time: the arrow matrix.

59
00:04:16,833 --> 00:04:20,938
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
